# Source list for requirements-lock.txt
# (pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-annotate --no-header --output-file=requirements-lock.txt requirements.in).
# This is the dependency surface environment.yml's `pip:` block lists directly;
# environment.yml just points at the compiled lock (`-r requirements-lock.txt`).
# Conda-managed dependencies (python, pytest, ...) are not here — they are pinned
# directly in environment.yml. See docs/standards/dependencies.md.
#
# requirements-lock.txt IS committed in this project (generated once the environment name
# and this source list were real — see MVP-000). Note for anyone reusing this file as a
# template elsewhere: pip-compile defaults its output to `requirements.txt` (input stem +
# `.txt`) when `--output-file` is omitted — always pass it explicitly, or you'll silently
# generate the wrong filename (this project hit exactly that once; see MVP-000's context).
#
# To update: edit a range here, then re-run
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-annotate --no-header --output-file=requirements-lock.txt requirements.in
# and commit both files together. CI fails if requirements-lock.txt drifts from this file.
# Prefer letting CI's own recompile (Linux, the actual deploy target) be authoritative over
# a local Windows recompile — Windows and Linux can silently resolve different transitive
# packages for the same requirements.in (see the example comment below); if CI's drift
# check fails after a local Windows edit, trust CI's diff over your own local output.

# Example of the platform-conditional-package gotcha this project hit twice (colorama via
# tqdm's Windows-only marker; tzdata via a pandas version that needed it on Windows but not
# Linux): when a package silently differs between a local Windows compile and CI's Linux
# compile, pin it here explicitly and unconditionally rather than letting a transitive
# marker decide it per platform. Delete this comment and the example line below once you
# have real dependencies and have hit (or ruled out) the same issue yourself.
# example-transitive-package==1.2.3

# Ruff is pinned exactly — its formatter/lint output changes between minor versions. Keep
# it on the same version as `rev` in .pre-commit-config.yaml.
ruff==0.16.7
pre-commit>=3,<5
# docs/standards/testing.md and ci.yml's "Run tests" job both assume pytest-cov (`pytest
# --cov`), but nothing had actually added it as a dependency until now — a real gap that
# was masked by GAP-E2-CICHAIN failing that same CI job earlier, before the coverage step
# ever ran. Discovered by actually running `pytest --cov` locally (MVP-001 Phase 2), not by
# reading the config.
pytest-cov>=5,<7

# SUMO mobility simulation engine (MVP-001 — see docs/plans/001-first-traffic-simulator.plan.md
# and ADR-006). Pinned exactly, all three at the same version deliberately: `traci`/`sumolib`
# are thin Python APIs over the `eclipse-sumo` binaries and must match the engine version they
# talk to, not just satisfy a loose range. Note the real PyPI name is `eclipse-sumo` —
# conda-forge's own package literally called `sumo` is an unrelated, different project
# (ab-initio solid-state-physics plotting tools); do not install that one.
# Licence: dual EPL-2.0/GPL-2.0-or-later — approved by the project owner 2026-09-16, see
# docs/standards/dependencies.md's allow-list.
eclipse-sumo==1.27.1
traci==1.27.1
sumolib==1.27.1
